# Draw - free reeds: harmonicas, accordions, melodica, harmonium and the
# reeds in pipes.
#
# Each note is a reed blown by the pressure across it (draw::FreeReed): it
# speaks, settles and stops by itself, its jet turbulent. A harmonica note is
# a hole instead (draw::HarpHole): two reeds in a channel and the player's
# mouth behind them, so bends come from the mouth. Reeds and bodies are
# fitted to recordings note by note (tools/draw_reference/fit.py), on the
# shape of the first twelve harmonics: accordion 5.2 dB rms (two reeds 25
# cents apart, as the recording's are; its recordings against each other
# 4.3), harmonica 7.3 (6.0), melodica 7.7 (7.2), harmonium 6.0, one
# accordion's registers rank by rank 6.9, brightness within about a
# semitone; the noise between the harmonics to within 5 dB (natural.py).
# The accordion sounds two reeds a note, 15 cents apart unless a patch says;
# registers choose 16', 8' and 4' ranks, as its switches do. A sheng's,
# sho's or khaen's reed sounds into a pipe (draw::PipeReed) that rings with
# it and filters it: the sheng's even harmonics 35 dB down.
#
# Sixty-six patches in seven families, plainest first:
#
#   accordion  Init, the bellows, wet and dry, registers, shaken, both hands (14)
#   button     bandoneon and concertina, both hands, slow, a jig (8)
#   harmonium  stops, a celeste, a drone box, a devotional and a parlour one (9)
#   melodica   soft, breathy, a lead, an echo, the bass melodica (7)
#   harmonica  keys, cupped, straight, chromatic, tremolo, octave, low, amped (12)
#   pipes      sheng, sho, khaen and pitch pipe (9)
#   odd        ranks no instrument has, a talking harp, bellows on the grid (7)
#
# Accordion and harmonium patches play the `chord` phrase, the others `tune`;
# Drone Box plays `drone`. Values are in each parameter's own units;
# `audition params Draw` lists them. A patch names only what it changes.
# Every patch sits at -23.1 on `loud` at its own note within 2 dB (-21.1
# Tierce Harmonium to -24.9 Sho Pipes); the cupped and low harps are the
# quiet end, the hard-blown accordions and harmoniums the loud.
#
# The left hand: Polka Accordion, Bandoneon Both Hands and Pumping Bellows
# set `bass` to 48, so the keys under C3 play the bass buttons (a bass
# note, its octave and a chord; the bandoneon's single notes). They play the
# `squeeze` phrase, a tune over the buttons on C2 to B2. A held button is
# about 2 dB over a held right-hand note (the bandoneon's 4).
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are
# numbered as in Draw.h's MacroDest: #1 pressure, #2 attack, #3 release,
# #4 set, #5 chamber, #6 air, #7 velocity, #8 volume, #9 detune, #10 cup,
# #11 vibrato, #12 vib rate, #13 shake, #14 words, #15 bass level,
# #16 pitch. Where a macro at full moves the level by more than 1.5 dB, a
# volume route takes it back out, as far as the volume knob goes: Bright
# and Hands on Low F Harp and Hands on Chord Harp stay 1.7-2.4 dB down, as
# their volumes are at the top. Set, chamber, air and detune are read when
# a note starts, so those macros change the next note.
#
# Word Harp needs words in the clip, and breath from a track needs another
# track: neither shows in a patch alone, so no patch here breathes from a
# track (alone it would be silent).
#
# What is still wrong:
#   - A harmonica's tone moves about half as much from moment to moment as a
#     played one's: more breath wander puts its bends out of tune.
#   - Hole 1 hardly bends and holes 8 to 10 bend a little less than a real
#     harp's; those notes play on a reed of their own instead.
#   - Overblows and overdraws play on a reed of their own: modelled, they
#     only speak in a narrow band of breath and mouth and wandered 50 cents.
#   - The top hole (C7) is too bright.
#   - The accordion's 3rd and 4th harmonics are about 10 dB strong; the
#     melodica's middle harmonics weak.
#   - The harmonium's bottom octave speaks weakly and flat, and its odd
#     harmonics don't arrive late as a real one's do; its patches slow the
#     bellows instead. Its even harmonics are 5 dB over the odd, not 8.
#   - Low reeds blown up slowly speak flat: a 16' rank under A2 with an
#     attack over about 150 ms reads 50-100 cents low, so the slow
#     bandoneon and the drone box keep their attacks near 110-120 ms.
#   - The bandoneon is fitted to one low note, 3 semitones bright; the
#     concertina is voiced by ear, with no recording to fit.
#   - The shaken patches warn of a wobble: that's the shake.
#   - The pipes are driven by their reeds and don't pull them: a pipe can't
#     choke a reed or lock it to a different note, as a real one can.
#   - The sho tunes A to 440, not its traditional 430; tune -40 for that.

machine Draw
role    tune

# --- accordion -------------------------------------------------------------------

patch Init family=accordion note=57 range=45..81 role=chord

patch "Soft Accordion" family=accordion note=57 range=45..81 role=chord
  # The bellows drawn gently, the reeds slow to speak.
  set macro1 "Bellows"
  set macro2 "Bright"
  set macro3 "Dark"
  set macro4 "Wet"
  set macro5 "Shake"
  set macro6 "Swell"
  set macro7 "Box"
  set macro8 "Air"
  mac1_d1  #1
  mac1_a1  0.4
  mac1_d2  #8
  mac1_a2  -0.175
  mac2_d1  #4
  mac2_a1  -0.4
  mac3_d1  #10
  mac3_a1  0.5
  mac4_d1  #9
  mac4_a1  0.3
  mac5_d1  #13
  mac5_a1  0.5
  mac6_d1  #2
  mac6_a1  0.35
  mac6_d2  #3
  mac6_a2  0.25
  mac7_d1  #5
  mac7_a1  0.5
  mac8_d1  #6
  mac8_a1  0.6
  pressure       0.3
  attack         140
  release        120

